A||N||3||DaveL||Used ECM's and Ignition Modules||||1||
Z||000000||DaveL||04-07-2000||03:57 PM||||Don't buy 'em. After all the horse hockey I just went through, I'll never buy another used electronic ignition part again. I had a mechanic that had me convinced I had a bad wiring problem, would take hours to fix. All it was, was that he put in a used computer that was no good. Then to make it even better, he put in another used one that was in even worse shape. I put in a new ECM myself, and the problems all disappeared! Now on to the front suspension and the AC.||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||04-07-2000||08:12 PM||||Dave, <P>Your problem wasn't caused by a used ECM. It was caused by an incompetant mech. Had he checked the ECM properly before installing it he would likely have seen it was bad. Cracked joints on the connectors are common and not hard to spot. They aren't hard to fix either as long as you are carefull and don't get sloppy.<P>Used ignition modules can be OK to as long as you know what to look for. I wouldn't use them in most GM's just because of the way they are built. I've reused many old Ford boxes with little problem.<P>Another problem with GM ignition parts is that hardly anyone in the car world really understand how to install them. You don't just screw them down and expect them to last.||||reg||1
Z||000002||DaveL||04-10-2000||10:25 AM||||Oh how right you are. I worked for GM for five years in the parts department. Used to run deliveries between dealers and body shops, garages etc.. But that was in the mid to late 70's. Things have changed ever so slightly. Say, do you know if ATF is good for the complexion and scalp? I was changing the auto trns. fluid & filter this weekend, and had an interesting? experience. I got all 16 or so bolts out of the trans. pan, and the pan wouldn't move. I tried prying lightly with a screwdriver to no avail. So I crawled far enough under the pan to see the front lip looking for somewhere to pry when the pan just dropped! Right on my chest. The fluid went in my face, my entire scalp. I was lying in a pretty large stain, probably looked like I had died right there! Threw out all the clothes, washed hair three times, no more dry scalp! I'm lucky I let the fluid cool down some before the change. Just a warning for anyone who cares. Had trouble getting the filter in place, then I figured out that if you take off the "O ring" and install it first, the filter slides right into place. Took almost 5 quarts to refill it.||||reg||3
Z||000003||theogre||04-10-2000||07:20 PM||||EEEEEEWwwwwww.........<P>last time I got my hair full of tranny oil I was working on an old chevy van. I washed it out with dish soap. Got it right out. then a regular wash and conditioner.||||reg||1
